00:13.606 --> 00:15.106 align:middle
January 28, 2026 CHAIR POWELL.

00:15.106 --> 00:15.416 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:15.416 --> 00:17.776 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:17.776 --> 00:22.006 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:22.006 --> 00:24.846 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:25.886 --> 00:28.886 align:middle
The U.S. economy expanded
at a solid pace last year

00:29.426 --> 00:32.426 align:middle
and is coming into
2026 on a firm footing.

00:33.866 --> 00:35.546 align:middle
While job gains have
remained low,

00:35.756 --> 00:38.926 align:middle
the unemployment rate has shown
some signs of stabilization,

00:39.476 --> 00:41.746 align:middle
and inflation remains
somewhat elevated.

00:43.606 --> 00:44.726 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:44.726 --> 00:46.816 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:46.816 --> 00:49.006 align:middle
to leave our policy
rate unchanged.

00:50.036 --> 00:53.126 align:middle
Having lowered our policy
rate by 75 basis points

00:53.226 --> 00:55.426 align:middle
over the course of our
previous three meetings,

00:56.066 --> 00:59.016 align:middle
we see the current stance of
monetary policy as appropriate

00:59.506 --> 01:02.506 align:middle
to promote progress toward
both our maximum-employment

01:02.506 --> 01:04.266 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation goals.

01:05.456 --> 01:07.426 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

01:07.426 --> 01:09.666 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:11.386 --> 01:12.736 align:middle
Available indicators suggest

01:12.736 --> 01:15.986 align:middle
that economic activity has
been expanding at a solid pace.

01:16.926 --> 01:18.706 align:middle
Consumer spending
has been resilient,

01:19.056 --> 01:21.576 align:middle
and business fixed investment
has continued to expand.

01:23.056 --> 01:24.466 align:middle
In contrast, activity

01:24.466 --> 01:26.356 align:middle
in the housing sector
has remained weak.

01:27.816 --> 01:28.796 align:middle
The temporary shutdown

01:28.796 --> 01:30.446 align:middle
of the federal government
likely weighed

01:30.446 --> 01:32.246 align:middle
on economic activity
last quarter,

01:32.796 --> 01:34.706 align:middle
but these effects
should be reversed

01:34.806 --> 01:37.396 align:middle
as the reopening boosts
growth this quarter.

01:39.396 --> 01:41.296 align:middle
In the labor market,
indicators suggest

01:41.296 --> 01:43.006 align:middle
that conditions may
be stabilizing

01:43.006 --> 01:44.896 align:middle
after a period of
gradual softening.

01:45.796 --> 01:48.436 align:middle
The unemployment rate was
4.4 percent in December

01:48.576 --> 01:50.716 align:middle
and has changed little
in recent months.

01:51.746 --> 01:53.326 align:middle
Job gains have remained low.

01:54.466 --> 01:57.486 align:middle
Total nonfarm payrolls
declined at an average pace

01:57.486 --> 02:00.116 align:middle
of 22,000 per month over
the last three months;

02:00.766 --> 02:04.006 align:middle
excluding government employment,
private payrolls rose

02:04.136 --> 02:06.446 align:middle
at an average pace
of 29,000 per month.

02:07.786 --> 02:10.146 align:middle
A good part of the slowing
in the pace of job growth

02:10.146 --> 02:12.996 align:middle
over the past year reflects
a decline in the growth

02:13.046 --> 02:15.956 align:middle
of the labor force due
to lower immigration

02:16.006 --> 02:17.406 align:middle
and labor force participation,

02:18.126 --> 02:20.796 align:middle
though labor demand has
clearly softened as well.

02:20.796 --> 02:23.006 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 2

02:23.006 --> 02:25.256 align:middle
of 24 Other indicators,
including openings, layoffs,

02:25.256 --> 02:27.046 align:middle
hiring, and nominal wage growth,

02:27.346 --> 02:29.196 align:middle
show little change
in recent months.

02:31.066 --> 02:33.376 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:33.376 --> 02:37.616 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:37.616 --> 02:39.416 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:41.016 --> 02:43.626 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index indicate

02:43.626 --> 02:46.746 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.9 percent

02:47.186 --> 02:49.726 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in December and that,

02:49.726 --> 02:52.086 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:52.356 --> 02:55.386 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
3.0 percent.

02:56.276 --> 02:59.396 align:middle
These elevated readings
largely reflect inflation

02:59.396 --> 03:01.426 align:middle
in the goods sector,
which has been boosted

03:01.426 --> 03:02.716 align:middle
by the effects of tariffs.

03:03.376 --> 03:06.706 align:middle
In contrast, disinflation
appears to be continuing

03:06.706 --> 03:07.646 align:middle
in the services sector.

03:08.756 --> 03:11.526 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have declined

03:11.526 --> 03:14.546 align:middle
from last year's peaks, as
reflected in both market-

03:14.586 --> 03:16.176 align:middle
and survey-based measures.

03:17.516 --> 03:20.696 align:middle
Most measures of longer-term
expectations remain consistent

03:20.696 --> 03:22.416 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:24.156 --> 03:27.836 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:27.876 --> 03:30.326 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:30.326 --> 03:31.326 align:middle
for the American people.

03:32.596 --> 03:35.016 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:35.016 --> 03:37.356 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:37.716 --> 03:39.816 align:middle
at 3½ to 3  percent.

03:40.776 --> 03:44.546 align:middle
Since last September, we have
lowered our policy rate 75 basis

03:44.546 --> 03:46.326 align:middle
points, or   of a
percentage point,

03:46.886 --> 03:49.766 align:middle
bringing it within a range of
plausible estimates of neutral.

03:50.636 --> 03:54.236 align:middle
This normalization of our policy
stance should help stabilize the

03:54.236 --> 03:56.406 align:middle
labor market while
allowing inflation

03:56.406 --> 03:59.736 align:middle
to resume its downward trend
toward 2 percent once the

03:59.736 --> 04:01.856 align:middle
effects of tariff increases
have passed through.

04:03.286 --> 04:06.496 align:middle
We are well positioned to
determine the extent and timing

04:06.676 --> 04:09.476 align:middle
of additional adjustments
to our policy rate based

04:09.476 --> 04:11.846 align:middle
on the incoming data,
the evolving outlook,

04:12.436 --> 04:13.586 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

04:14.666 --> 04:17.106 align:middle
Monetary policy is not
on a preset course,

04:17.656 --> 04:19.546 align:middle
and we will make our decisions
on a meeting-by-meeting basis.

04:19.546 --> 04:21.946 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 3

04:21.946 --> 04:24.166 align:middle
of 24 To conclude, the Fed
has been assigned two goals

04:24.166 --> 04:27.186 align:middle
for monetary policy: maximum
employment and stable prices.

04:27.806 --> 04:30.116 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

04:30.606 --> 04:33.376 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

04:33.796 --> 04:36.306 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

04:36.446 --> 04:37.046 align:middle
well anchored.

04:38.156 --> 04:39.376 align:middle
Our success in delivering

04:39.376 --> 04:41.826 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

04:42.496 --> 04:45.316 align:middle
We at the Fed will continue to
do our jobs with objectivity,

04:45.696 --> 04:48.516 align:middle
integrity, and a deep commitment
to serve the American people.

04:49.706 --> 04:50.086 align:middle
Thank you.

04:50.346 --> 04:51.706 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

04:57.346 --> 04:58.196 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

04:58.366 --> 05:00.496 align:middle
Hi. Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

05:00.556 --> 05:00.996 align:middle
Thank you.

05:01.736 --> 05:07.266 align:middle
I wanted to ask-you attended the
Supreme Court hearing last week

05:07.266 --> 05:08.616 align:middle
on the Lisa Cook case,

05:08.616 --> 05:11.216 align:middle
and Treasury Secretary
Scott Bessent criticized

05:11.216 --> 05:12.096 align:middle
that as political.

05:12.716 --> 05:16.346 align:middle
Can you say why you attended and
what you would say in response

05:16.406 --> 05:17.726 align:middle
to the secretary's criticism?

05:17.796 --> 05:18.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

05:18.976 --> 05:22.386 align:middle
So let me start with, I
don't respond to comments

05:22.416 --> 05:24.656 align:middle
by other officials,
whoever they may be.

05:24.656 --> 05:26.606 align:middle
It's just not appropriate
to do that.

05:26.606 --> 05:28.516 align:middle
I will tell you why I attended.

05:28.646 --> 05:34.376 align:middle
I would say that that case is
perhaps the most important legal

05:34.376 --> 05:37.236 align:middle
case in the Fed's
113-year history.

05:37.866 --> 05:41.166 align:middle
And as I thought about it,
I thought it might be hard

05:41.166 --> 05:42.436 align:middle
to explain why I didn't attend.

05:42.436 --> 05:46.016 align:middle
In addition, Paul Volcker went

05:46.016 --> 05:49.256 align:middle
to a Supreme Court case famously
in, I guess, 1985 or so,

05:49.756 --> 05:51.386 align:middle
so it's precedented,

05:51.606 --> 05:54.066 align:middle
and I thought it was an
appropriate thing, and I did it.

05:54.896 --> 05:55.406 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

05:55.406 --> 05:56.746 align:middle
Great. And then just
quickly follow on,

05:57.146 --> 06:01.336 align:middle
the job market-you
mentioned last month

06:01.506 --> 06:04.246 align:middle
that the household
survey might be distorted,

06:04.316 --> 06:09.436 align:middle
and you also mentioned the
potential for overcounting jobs,

06:10.346 --> 06:13.896 align:middle
which would suggest that we're
still in a negative hiring pace.

06:14.046 --> 06:17.426 align:middle
So do you see the drop in the
unemployment rate as solid?

06:17.426 --> 06:18.546 align:middle
And I'm just-what's
the basis for saying

06:18.546 --> 06:20.286 align:middle
that things have stabilized?

06:20.426 --> 06:20.656 align:middle
Thank you.

06:20.656 --> 06:24.356 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 4

06:24.356 --> 06:24.976 align:middle
of 24 CHAIR POWELL.

06:24.976 --> 06:25.996 align:middle
So, yeah, really two questions.

06:25.996 --> 06:29.536 align:middle
One is-so we're getting
through the distortions

06:29.536 --> 06:30.866 align:middle
in the data from the shutdown.

06:31.356 --> 06:33.256 align:middle
What-however big they
were in November,

06:33.256 --> 06:34.496 align:middle
they're smaller in December.

06:34.496 --> 06:35.536 align:middle
So we're getting to a place

06:35.536 --> 06:37.146 align:middle
where they're no
longer material.

06:37.326 --> 06:40.046 align:middle
They're still there, but
it's a tweak here and there.

06:40.456 --> 06:44.536 align:middle
The reason why we-why we
changed the statement,

06:44.626 --> 06:50.896 align:middle
let me pull it out, was, was
simply, it's-it used to say that

06:51.056 --> 06:52.406 align:middle
"judges the downside risks

06:52.406 --> 06:54.186 align:middle
to employment rose
in recent months."

06:54.626 --> 06:58.626 align:middle
So we saw data coming in
which suggests some signs

06:58.626 --> 07:00.906 align:middle
of stabilization-I wouldn't
go too far with that,

07:00.956 --> 07:02.446 align:middle
but some signs of stabilization.

07:02.446 --> 07:05.186 align:middle
There are also some signs
of continued cooling.

07:05.636 --> 07:08.766 align:middle
And so we thought that was no
longer an accurate description

07:08.766 --> 07:09.456 align:middle
of the data.

07:09.836 --> 07:11.636 align:middle
In addition, the outlook

07:11.636 --> 07:15.786 align:middle
for economic activity has
improved-clearly improved-since

07:15.786 --> 07:19.646 align:middle
the last meeting, and that
should matter for labor demand

07:19.646 --> 07:21.686 align:middle
and for employment over time.

07:21.686 --> 07:24.246 align:middle
So, for those two reasons,
we thought we would take

07:24.246 --> 07:25.396 align:middle
that language out
of the statement.

07:27.206 --> 07:29.596 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:30.816 --> 07:31.926 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

07:32.256 --> 07:33.816 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

07:34.506 --> 07:36.746 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've
generally avoided engaging

07:36.746 --> 07:38.506 align:middle
with political controversies
directly,

07:38.506 --> 07:41.856 align:middle
and the video statement on
January 11 was a departure.

07:42.576 --> 07:43.696 align:middle
What made this different,

07:43.696 --> 07:46.146 align:middle
and are you concerned it could
draw the institution further

07:46.146 --> 07:47.286 align:middle
into political debates?

07:47.286 --> 07:47.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:47.846 --> 07:52.876 align:middle
So today on-I'm simply going
to refer you to the statement

07:52.876 --> 07:54.246 align:middle
that I made on January 11.

07:54.406 --> 07:56.856 align:middle
I'm not going to expand
on it or repeat it.

07:57.026 --> 08:00.556 align:middle
So I'm just not going
to-this is really

08:00.606 --> 08:03.706 align:middle
about the press conference
and the economy

08:03.706 --> 08:06.336 align:middle
and what we did today,
but-and some ancillaries.

08:06.336 --> 08:07.546 align:middle
But I'm not going to
be getting into that.

08:07.546 --> 08:07.876 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:07.916 --> 08:10.686 align:middle
Can you say whether the Fed
has responded to the subpoenas?

08:10.686 --> 08:11.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:11.266 --> 08:12.456 align:middle
I have nothing for
you on that today.

08:12.456 --> 08:15.886 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 5

08:15.886 --> 08:18.136 align:middle
of 24 MICHELLE SMITH.

08:18.656 --> 08:19.026 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

08:19.116 --> 08:20.986 align:middle
Michael McKee from
Bloomberg Radio TV.

08:21.446 --> 08:24.456 align:middle
Have you made a decision
on whether you would remain

08:24.456 --> 08:27.456 align:middle
as a Governor of the
Federal Reserve, and, if so,

08:27.776 --> 08:28.976 align:middle
would you tell us what it is?

08:29.356 --> 08:32.476 align:middle
And, if not, when might
we anticipate a decision?

08:32.726 --> 08:33.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:33.516 --> 08:37.116 align:middle
No, and I really, once
again, have nothing for you

08:37.116 --> 08:38.826 align:middle
on that today-that either.

08:40.096 --> 08:40.986 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

08:40.986 --> 08:44.176 align:middle
Why would you want to leave at
all, under the circumstances?

08:45.066 --> 08:45.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:46.246 --> 08:48.446 align:middle
Again, I don't want
to get into this.

08:48.446 --> 08:51.436 align:middle
It's not something
I'm-there's a time and place

08:51.436 --> 08:54.966 align:middle
for these questions, but
not something I'm going

08:54.966 --> 08:55.906 align:middle
to be getting into today.

08:56.426 --> 08:56.726 align:middle
Thank you.

08:57.216 --> 08:57.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:58.186 --> 09:00.286 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

09:00.856 --> 09:01.116 align:middle
Thank you.

09:01.116 --> 09:02.426 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

09:02.426 --> 09:06.546 align:middle
Thanks a lot for taking my
questions-and another one

09:06.546 --> 09:07.976 align:middle
that might lead to
a similar answer.

09:08.866 --> 09:09.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:09.656 --> 09:10.626 align:middle
Give it a try.

09:11.356 --> 09:13.376 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

09:13.556 --> 09:17.356 align:middle
We've seen quite big movements
in the dollar over recent days.

09:17.506 --> 09:20.596 align:middle
What do you think is driving
the U.S. currency lower?

09:21.316 --> 09:24.286 align:middle
And have you been at all
concerned just by the extent

09:24.286 --> 09:26.256 align:middle
of the volatility
we've seen this week?

09:26.566 --> 09:26.876 align:middle
Thank you.

09:26.966 --> 09:27.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:27.566 --> 09:28.856 align:middle
So, Claire, as you
probably know,

09:29.016 --> 09:30.586 align:middle
we don't comment on the dollar.

09:30.586 --> 09:31.606 align:middle
Really, the Administration,

09:31.606 --> 09:33.166 align:middle
and especially the
Treasury Department,

09:33.166 --> 09:36.836 align:middle
has the job of oversight
over the currency

09:36.986 --> 09:38.646 align:middle
and exchange rates
and all of that.

09:38.646 --> 09:39.586 align:middle
We don't comment on that.

09:39.646 --> 09:42.086 align:middle
It's not our-not our role.

09:42.176 --> 09:44.086 align:middle
So I have nothing for you.

09:44.086 --> 09:44.546 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

09:44.546 --> 09:46.296 align:middle
But, I mean, what's your
view on the market movements?

09:46.296 --> 09:47.576 align:middle
I mean, what do you
think's behind them?

09:47.576 --> 09:49.576 align:middle
Is it asset managers
diversifying?

09:49.576 --> 09:51.986 align:middle
Is it- January 28, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

09:51.986 --> 09:53.286 align:middle
Page 6 of 24 CHAIR POWELL.

09:53.286 --> 09:54.486 align:middle
Yeah, I just don't-we don't
talk about the dollar.

09:54.486 --> 09:56.366 align:middle
We don't talk about
what moves it around.

09:56.366 --> 09:58.346 align:middle
It's just-it's just not
appropriate for us to do so.

09:58.346 --> 10:02.406 align:middle
Really, the Treasury Department
has that-it's their, their role,

10:02.406 --> 10:04.396 align:middle
their bailiwick,
and we stay off it.

10:04.396 --> 10:07.026 align:middle
We do monetary policy
and some other things,

10:07.066 --> 10:08.526 align:middle
but we don't-we don't
comment on the dollar.

10:08.756 --> 10:10.136 align:middle
Sorry. MICHELLE SMITH.

10:10.466 --> 10:12.616 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

10:13.086 --> 10:13.806 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

10:13.806 --> 10:14.946 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

10:14.946 --> 10:17.696 align:middle
Obviously no SEP in this
meeting, but in light

10:17.696 --> 10:19.846 align:middle
of the slight firming up of
the language around growth

10:19.846 --> 10:21.846 align:middle
in the labor market in the
statement, should we assume

10:21.846 --> 10:24.406 align:middle
that the timeline for any
further rate cuts is pushed

10:24.406 --> 10:26.106 align:middle
back, compared to what people
might have thought in December?

10:26.106 --> 10:28.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:28.206 --> 10:31.426 align:middle
Well, first of all, if you
look at the incoming data

10:31.426 --> 10:34.466 align:middle
since the last meeting,
clear improvement

10:34.466 --> 10:35.606 align:middle
in the outlook for growth.

10:35.796 --> 10:38.036 align:middle
The data have come
in, and sentiment,

10:38.036 --> 10:40.486 align:middle
the Beige Book-everything-comes
in suggesting

10:40.486 --> 10:45.266 align:middle
that this year starts off on
a solid footing for growth.

10:45.986 --> 10:49.166 align:middle
Inflation performed about as
expected, and, as I mentioned,

10:49.166 --> 10:50.826 align:middle
some of the labor
market data came

10:50.826 --> 10:55.106 align:middle
in suggesting evidence
of stabilization.

10:55.106 --> 10:58.546 align:middle
So it's overall a
stronger forecast, really,

10:59.036 --> 10:59.906 align:middle
if that's your question.

11:00.386 --> 11:02.446 align:middle
I'm not sure I answered
your question.

11:02.636 --> 11:03.616 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

11:03.616 --> 11:07.346 align:middle
But in terms of timing or
pacing of any additional easing.

11:07.346 --> 11:07.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:07.756 --> 11:11.286 align:middle
So we haven't made-what
we'd say about this was

11:11.286 --> 11:14.906 align:middle
that after this meeting, after
the three recent rate cuts,

11:14.906 --> 11:17.806 align:middle
we're well positioned to
address the risks that we face

11:17.806 --> 11:20.166 align:middle
on both sides of our dual
mandate, and we'll continue

11:20.166 --> 11:22.726 align:middle
to make our decisions
meeting by meeting based

11:22.726 --> 11:24.776 align:middle
on the incoming data
implications for-and the

11:24.776 --> 11:27.416 align:middle
implications for the outlook
and the balance of risks.

11:27.656 --> 11:29.666 align:middle
Haven't made any decisions
about future meetings,

11:30.096 --> 11:33.136 align:middle
but the economy is
growing at a solid pace,

11:33.466 --> 11:35.406 align:middle
the unemployment rate
has been broadly stable,

11:35.916 --> 11:37.716 align:middle
and inflation remains
somewhat elevated.

11:37.716 --> 11:40.356 align:middle
So we'll be looking
to our goal variables

11:40.356 --> 11:42.156 align:middle
and letting the data
light the way for us.

11:42.266 --> 11:47.456 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 7

11:47.456 --> 11:48.606 align:middle
of 24 MICHELLE SMITH.

11:48.646 --> 11:49.746 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

11:49.746 --> 11:50.446 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

11:50.446 --> 11:55.126 align:middle
And sticking with questions you
might answer-thank you-you had

11:55.126 --> 11:59.656 align:middle
