the current policy rate being

11:59.656 --> 12:01.176 align:middle
at the higher end of neutral.

12:01.176 --> 12:03.776 align:middle
And if you look at the
longer run of the SEP

12:03.776 --> 12:04.716 align:middle
and the longer-run rate,

12:04.716 --> 12:08.956 align:middle
16 of 19 officials are actually
below there on their long run.

12:09.646 --> 12:12.076 align:middle
Is the Fed still in the
process of bringing it

12:12.116 --> 12:14.606 align:middle
down towards a middle
range of neutral?

12:15.156 --> 12:16.266 align:middle
And what would it
take to get there?

12:16.266 --> 12:17.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:17.006 --> 12:23.136 align:middle
So I-the count I did was that 4
of the 19 were at or above that.

12:23.466 --> 12:26.396 align:middle
And maybe I missed by
1-I thought it was 4.

12:26.876 --> 12:30.176 align:middle
And if you look at
the dealer survey,

12:30.586 --> 12:33.356 align:middle
it was 10 out of 58
were at or above.

12:33.356 --> 12:37.796 align:middle
So you're right, it's the
high-higher end of the range.

12:37.796 --> 12:40.676 align:middle
What we say is it's within the
range of plausible estimates.

12:40.676 --> 12:42.366 align:middle
This is the higher
end of that range,

12:42.366 --> 12:44.976 align:middle
but it's-so for some people,
I think, it's neutral.

12:45.406 --> 12:47.946 align:middle
I think-and many of my
colleagues think-it's hard

12:47.946 --> 12:50.266 align:middle
to look at the incoming
data and say

12:50.266 --> 12:53.196 align:middle
that policy's significantly
restrictive at this time.

12:53.196 --> 12:55.886 align:middle
It may be-it may be
sort of loosely neutral,

12:55.886 --> 12:57.556 align:middle
or it may be somewhat
restrictive.

12:57.556 --> 12:59.606 align:middle
It's in the eye of the
beholder, and, of course,

12:59.606 --> 13:01.676 align:middle
no one knows with any precision.

13:01.676 --> 13:04.146 align:middle
You've got a follow-up?

13:04.546 --> 13:05.106 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:05.516 --> 13:08.306 align:middle
I get what you're saying,

13:08.506 --> 13:12.236 align:middle
but I-some officials have
described the Fed being

13:12.236 --> 13:15.676 align:middle
in a mode of bringing it
down eventually, over time.

13:15.676 --> 13:18.626 align:middle
Are you still in that mode, or
is this a place to hang out?

13:18.776 --> 13:19.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:19.186 --> 13:23.436 align:middle
Yeah, no, I would say, if you
look at the SEP from December,

13:23.976 --> 13:27.136 align:middle
you-most people had
additional normalization.

13:27.536 --> 13:29.546 align:middle
But at the same time,
we've done a lot

13:29.546 --> 13:30.946 align:middle
of the process of normalizing.

13:30.946 --> 13:32.126 align:middle
A good piece of it is done,

13:32.126 --> 13:34.806 align:middle
with 75 basis points
and, before that, 100.

13:34.806 --> 13:36.856 align:middle
It's 175 basis points we've cut

13:37.426 --> 13:40.716 align:middle
since we began cutting
in September of 2024.

13:41.006 --> 13:41.866 align:middle
So you've moved now

13:41.866 --> 13:43.296 align:middle
to 3 point-fed funds is
running just a little below

13:43.516 --> 13:45.076 align:middle
3.65 percent.

13:45.076 --> 13:46.256 align:middle
So you've moved a
good way, January 28,

13:46.256 --> 13:50.456 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 8 of 24

13:50.746 --> 13:52.656 align:middle
and we think we're
well positioned here

13:52.886 --> 13:55.096 align:middle
to watch how the economy
performs, look at the data.

13:55.736 --> 13:57.666 align:middle
We're not making decisions
about future meetings,

13:57.666 --> 13:59.266 align:middle
but we do think we're
well positioned

13:59.266 --> 14:02.486 align:middle
after those three cuts to
let the data speak to us.

14:02.486 --> 14:03.026 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:03.026 --> 14:05.096 align:middle
Amara. AMARA OMEOKWE.

14:06.716 --> 14:07.846 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

14:07.846 --> 14:09.216 align:middle
Amara Omeokwe from
Bloomberg News.

14:09.636 --> 14:12.646 align:middle
To what extent did the Committee
discuss the possibility

14:12.646 --> 14:14.586 align:middle
of cutting at this
meeting or in March?

14:15.026 --> 14:16.746 align:middle
And how are you all
thinking about the conditions

14:16.776 --> 14:18.036 align:middle
that would merit
another rate cut?

14:18.356 --> 14:20.296 align:middle
Is there broad agreement
on the Committee

14:20.296 --> 14:21.046 align:middle
about what it would take?

14:21.046 --> 14:22.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:22.926 --> 14:25.376 align:middle
So there was broad
support on the Committee

14:25.616 --> 14:28.946 align:middle
for holding today-"broad,"
I would say,

14:29.136 --> 14:30.296 align:middle
including among nonvoters.

14:30.856 --> 14:31.656 align:middle
So that's where that was.

14:31.656 --> 14:34.986 align:middle
Of course, some people did
want to cut and dissented.

14:35.546 --> 14:41.016 align:middle
But the Committee [was] pretty
broadly for holding today.

14:41.966 --> 14:46.076 align:middle
We're not trying to
articulate a test for what-for

14:46.076 --> 14:48.386 align:middle
when to next cut, or whether
to cut at the next meeting.

14:48.956 --> 14:51.746 align:middle
What we're saying is,
we're well positioned

14:52.416 --> 14:54.586 align:middle
as we make decisions
meeting by meeting,

14:54.586 --> 14:55.896 align:middle
looking at the incoming data,

14:56.246 --> 14:58.376 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and all that.

14:58.376 --> 15:04.246 align:middle
And we're in a position where
we have to-we still have some

15:04.246 --> 15:07.256 align:middle
tension between employment
and inflation,

15:07.256 --> 15:08.276 align:middle
but it's less than it was.

15:08.276 --> 15:11.916 align:middle
I think that the upside risks to
inflation and the downside risks

15:11.916 --> 15:14.076 align:middle
to employment have probably
both diminished a bit,

15:14.776 --> 15:16.766 align:middle
so we'll be looking at that.

15:16.766 --> 15:20.276 align:middle
It's about how you weigh
the risks to the two goals

15:20.556 --> 15:22.676 align:middle
and how big those-and
quantify them.

15:22.676 --> 15:24.216 align:middle
And so there are different
views on the Committee,

15:24.216 --> 15:26.946 align:middle
and we'll find our way
forward as the data evolve.

15:29.046 --> 15:31.076 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:31.076 --> 15:32.186 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

15:32.186 --> 15:33.076 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

15:33.076 --> 15:34.906 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

15:35.216 --> 15:37.416 align:middle
Has the effects of
tariffs already moved

15:37.416 --> 15:38.336 align:middle
through the economy on prices?

15:38.336 --> 15:41.806 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 9

15:41.806 --> 15:42.596 align:middle
of 24 CHAIR POWELL.

15:42.596 --> 15:42.936 align:middle
A lot of it has.

15:42.936 --> 15:45.776 align:middle
So, basically, there are
many different estimates,

15:45.776 --> 15:47.626 align:middle
and they're all highly
uncertain.

15:47.626 --> 15:52.656 align:middle
But most of the overrun in
goods prices is from tariffs.

15:52.856 --> 15:55.276 align:middle
And that's actually good
news, because if it weren't

15:55.276 --> 15:58.296 align:middle
from tariffs, it might
mean it's from demand,

15:58.366 --> 16:01.116 align:middle
and that's a harder
problem to solve.

16:01.116 --> 16:04.636 align:middle
We do think tariffs are
likely to move through

16:04.716 --> 16:06.266 align:middle
and be a one-time
price increase.

16:06.266 --> 16:10.396 align:middle
So most of the overshoot, if
you were to take that out,

16:10.396 --> 16:14.086 align:middle
you'd get-you would-I mean,
inflation, core PCE inflation,

16:14.086 --> 16:17.906 align:middle
is running just a bit above
2 percent ex the effects

16:17.906 --> 16:19.106 align:middle
of tariffs on goods.

16:19.656 --> 16:22.326 align:middle
And the other good news is
if you look away from goods

16:22.326 --> 16:25.666 align:middle
and look at services, you
do see ongoing disinflation

16:25.666 --> 16:27.436 align:middle
in all the categories
of services.

16:27.956 --> 16:29.286 align:middle
So that's a healthy development.

16:29.336 --> 16:30.426 align:middle
So that's what's going on.

16:30.716 --> 16:34.886 align:middle
The expectation is that
we will see the effects

16:34.946 --> 16:39.546 align:middle
of tariffs flowing through
goods prices, peaking,

16:39.546 --> 16:40.756 align:middle
and then starting to come down,

16:40.756 --> 16:44.946 align:middle
assuming there are no new major
tariff increases that are begun.

16:45.046 --> 16:48.016 align:middle
And that's what we expect to see
over the course of this year.

16:48.186 --> 16:51.726 align:middle
If we see that-if we see
that, that would be something

16:51.726 --> 16:54.506 align:middle
that tells us that we
can-we can loosen policy.

16:54.796 --> 16:56.426 align:middle
Also, if we see something
that suggests

16:56.426 --> 16:58.746 align:middle
that the labor market
is not stabilizing,

16:58.746 --> 17:01.916 align:middle
that in fact it's-the
downside risks reemerge,

17:01.916 --> 17:05.106 align:middle
or the data just get worse, we'd
have to look at both of those.

17:05.106 --> 17:06.316 align:middle
We have a two-sided mandate.

17:06.416 --> 17:07.126 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

17:07.256 --> 17:10.336 align:middle
If I could-if President Trump
does pick a new Fed Chairman

17:10.336 --> 17:12.636 align:middle
before May, what
does that look like?

17:12.636 --> 17:14.106 align:middle
How would you work with
that person, and what does

17:14.106 --> 17:15.206 align:middle
that transition period
look like?

17:15.206 --> 17:15.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:15.566 --> 17:17.706 align:middle
I don't have anything
for you on that.

17:17.986 --> 17:22.226 align:middle
I-that will depend
on Congress's actions

17:22.226 --> 17:24.146 align:middle
and things I can't speculate on.

17:25.606 --> 17:26.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:26.996 --> 17:28.186 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

17:28.186 --> 17:29.126 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

17:29.126 --> 17:30.746 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

17:31.376 --> 17:33.446 align:middle
Given the changes in the
statement and all you've said

17:33.446 --> 17:35.286 align:middle
so far, is it fair to
describe January 28,

17:35.286 --> 17:37.876 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 10

17:37.876 --> 17:39.636 align:middle
of 24 risks to your-to
both sides of the mandate

17:39.636 --> 17:40.686 align:middle
as roughly balanced right now?

17:40.686 --> 17:42.446 align:middle
And is the next move
necessarily down?

17:44.016 --> 17:46.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:46.036 --> 17:51.766 align:middle
I'd say that the upside-again,
the upside risks to inflation

17:51.766 --> 17:55.556 align:middle
and the downside risks to
employment have diminished.

17:56.156 --> 17:57.326 align:middle
But they still exist.

17:57.326 --> 17:59.476 align:middle
So there's still some
tension between the mandates.

18:00.096 --> 18:01.466 align:middle
Are they fully in balance?

18:02.406 --> 18:04.026 align:middle
Hard to say.

18:04.326 --> 18:04.946 align:middle
Hard to say.

18:05.456 --> 18:08.556 align:middle
And we-again, we think our
policy is in a good place.

18:09.196 --> 18:14.546 align:middle
I've just discussed reasons
why we might change our policy,

18:15.066 --> 18:17.196 align:middle
and we'll just have to
see how the data lead us.

18:17.546 --> 18:18.256 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

18:18.256 --> 18:21.496 align:middle
I'm wondering, a minute ago you
said you felt expectations were

18:21.496 --> 18:24.306 align:middle
consistent with your-with
your mandate, that the 2-

18:24.306 --> 18:27.046 align:middle
and the 10-year breakevens
have moved quite notably

18:27.046 --> 18:30.286 align:middle
in the last couple
of weeks, I believe.

18:30.286 --> 18:32.086 align:middle
Is there any concern
on that front?

18:32.276 --> 18:33.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:33.686 --> 18:37.696 align:middle
I mean, I recently looked
at all of the-both survey-

18:37.696 --> 18:41.466 align:middle
and market-based short-term
inflation expectations have come

18:41.466 --> 18:42.856 align:middle
way down.

18:42.856 --> 18:45.376 align:middle
They were in a good place at
the beginning of last year,

18:45.376 --> 18:47.476 align:middle
they spiked around
Liberation Day,

18:47.666 --> 18:50.206 align:middle
and now they've fully retraced
in the last few months.

18:50.206 --> 18:51.176 align:middle
So that's very comforting.

18:51.176 --> 18:54.436 align:middle
In the longer term, inflation
expectations have remained

18:54.996 --> 18:56.766 align:middle
in places that are
very consistent

18:56.766 --> 18:58.146 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation
over time.

18:58.296 --> 19:01.006 align:middle
So expectations are-have
been solid,

19:01.006 --> 19:04.116 align:middle
and they reflect
confidence in the return

19:04.116 --> 19:04.936 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation.

19:06.646 --> 19:07.596 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:07.596 --> 19:11.406 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

19:11.406 --> 19:13.246 align:middle
Apologies if this is a
little bit repetitive,

19:13.246 --> 19:16.596 align:middle
but in the past you've said that
the reason you cut rates was

19:16.666 --> 19:19.046 align:middle
that the risk to the labor
market was greater than the risk

19:19.046 --> 19:20.006 align:middle
to the inflation side.

19:20.296 --> 19:21.216 align:middle
Is that still true?

19:23.366 --> 19:23.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:23.966 --> 19:25.736 align:middle
We haven't made-you're right,

19:25.856 --> 19:30.446 align:middle
we saw the labor market
weakening, and we acted.

19:31.386 --> 19:33.336 align:middle
And I think that was the
appropriate thing to do.

19:33.336 --> 19:35.356 align:middle
We have a-we will always
act to address what we see

19:35.356 --> 19:36.346 align:middle
as the economy moving
away from our goals.

19:36.346 --> 19:38.606 align:middle
Risks to both of January 28,

19:38.606 --> 19:42.536 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 11

19:42.626 --> 19:45.256 align:middle
of 24 the variables are
a little less-I think

19:45.256 --> 19:46.776 align:middle
that the upside risks
to inflation, again,

19:46.776 --> 19:48.346 align:middle
a little bit less,
and the downside risks

19:48.346 --> 19:49.956 align:middle
to employment a little less.

19:50.776 --> 19:56.006 align:middle
I just would say that I'm not
making a judgment about how one

19:56.006 --> 20:01.406 align:middle
of them is more at risk than
the other-just that the risks

20:01.486 --> 20:03.186 align:middle
to both of them have diminished.

20:03.276 --> 20:03.616 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

20:03.616 --> 20:04.456 align:middle
Okay. Thanks.

20:04.496 --> 20:06.836 align:middle
BIS wrote a paper last
summer which concluded

20:06.836 --> 20:10.126 align:middle
that global investors were
hedging their dollar exposures,

20:10.126 --> 20:12.656 align:middle
in ways that previously
they hadn't,

20:12.726 --> 20:14.296 align:middle
because of policy uncertainty.

20:14.666 --> 20:15.746 align:middle
Do you agree with BIS?

20:15.746 --> 20:16.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:16.606 --> 20:19.336 align:middle
We really don't see
much at all about that,

20:19.366 --> 20:21.386 align:middle
that whole-that whole story.

20:21.386 --> 20:24.006 align:middle
There's just not a lot
of data that suggests

20:24.006 --> 20:24.876 align:middle
that there's much to that.

20:25.916 --> 20:26.876 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

20:27.166 --> 20:28.976 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

20:28.976 --> 20:29.776 align:middle
Ana. ANA SWANSON.

20:29.776 --> 20:31.216 align:middle
Ana Swanson with
the New York Times.

20:31.276 --> 20:33.816 align:middle
Can you talk more about
what you would need to see

20:33.816 --> 20:37.576 align:middle
in the labor market to conclude
it's time to resume easing?

20:37.766 --> 20:39.776 align:middle
Do you need to see
further deterioration

20:39.976 --> 20:42.146 align:middle
on the labor market,
or would it be enough

20:42.146 --> 20:43.206 align:middle
for inflation to soften?

20:43.206 --> 20:43.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:43.506 --> 20:45.466 align:middle
So we'll always be
looking at both things,

20:45.806 --> 20:48.396 align:middle
and so there could be
combinations, infinite numbers

20:48.396 --> 20:50.376 align:middle
of combinations, that would
cause us to want to move.

20:50.376 --> 20:53.406 align:middle
Certainly, a weakening labor
market would be an argument

20:53.406 --> 20:54.206 align:middle
for loosening.

20:54.776 --> 20:56.226 align:middle
But what's happening
with inflation?

20:56.226 --> 20:58.276 align:middle
If inflation were at the
same time getting worse,

20:58.686 --> 21:01.096 align:middle
you just have a very
difficult situation there.

21:01.496 --> 21:02.626 align:middle
So we'll be looking at both.

21:03.306 --> 21:05.826 align:middle
Clearly, a weakening labor
market calls for cutting;

21:05.826 --> 21:07.076 align:middle
a stronger labor market says

21:07.076 --> 21:08.386 align:middle
that the rates are
in a good place.

21:08.386 --> 21:09.546 align:middle
We'll have to be making
similar judgments,

21:09.546 --> 21:10.376 align:middle
too, on inflation, though.

21:10.376 --> 21:12.796 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 12

21:12.796 --> 21:14.276 align:middle
of 24 ANA SWANSON.

21:14.276 --> 21:15.756 align:middle
And if inflation
does pick back up

21:15.756 --> 21:18.056 align:middle
and the labor market
doesn't show further signs

21:18.056 --> 21:19.966 align:middle
of deterioration,
is there a chance

21:19.966 --> 21:21.136 align:middle
that you could raise rates,

21:21.136 --> 21:22.716 align:middle
rather than simply
remaining on hold?

21:22.716 --> 21:24.486 align:middle
What would you need to
see to consider hiking?

21:24.716 --> 21:24.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:24.986 --> 21:26.316 align:middle
We don't take things
off the table,

21:26.316 --> 21:29.436 align:middle
but it isn't anybody's base
case right now-anybody's base

21:29.436 --> 21:32.766 align:middle
case-that the next move
will be a rate hike.

21:32.856 --> 21:36.726 align:middle
But, ultimately, we'll do what
we think is the right thing.

21:36.726 --> 21:39.546 align:middle
But that's not where people's
expectations are right now.

21:41.106 --> 21:42.276 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:42.276 --> 21:44.466 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

21:45.286 --> 21:46.716 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

21:46.716 --> 21:50.136 align:middle
I wanted to ask, you've talked
in the past about concerns

21:50.136 --> 21:52.496 align:middle
about the U.S. fiscal
trajectory, and we've seen

21:52.496 --> 21:55.366 align:middle
in the Japanese bond market
a lot of turmoil recently,

21:55.906 --> 21:58.026 align:middle
in part due to concerns
over their fiscal

21:58.096 --> 21:59.466 align:middle
and long-term economic outlook.

21:59.516 --> 22:02.786 align:middle
So do you worry that the
U.S. could, at some point,

22:02.786 --> 22:05.326 align:middle
find itself in a similar
situation to Japan,

22:05.326 --> 22:07.296 align:middle
whether for fiscal or
demographic reasons?

22:07.586 --> 22:08.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:08.016 --> 22:12.416 align:middle
Over time, you've seen that U.S.
rates have remained pretty-they

22:12.416 --> 22:14.826 align:middle
haven't moved a lot,
really for a while.

22:14.826 --> 22:15.946 align:middle
But they haven't moved a lot

22:16.526 --> 22:19.016 align:middle
because of what's been
happening in Japan.

22:19.146 --> 22:22.146 align:middle
So it's more of an
"over time" thing.

22:22.526 --> 22:27.246 align:middle
The U.S. federal budget
deficit is uncontroversially

22:27.246 --> 22:28.316 align:middle
on an unsustainable path.

22:28.666 --> 22:31.096 align:middle
The level of debt is not
unsustainable-it's very much

22:31.096 --> 22:33.586 align:middle
sustainable-but the
path is unsustainable.

22:34.066 --> 22:35.676 align:middle
And the sooner we work
on it, the better.

22:35.916 --> 22:40.066 align:middle
But right now we're
running a very large deficit

22:40.176 --> 22:41.846 align:middle
at essentially full employment,

22:42.446 --> 22:45.256 align:middle
and so the fiscal picture
needs to be addressed.

22:45.766 --> 22:47.206 align:middle
And it's not really
being addressed.

22:47.276 --> 22:49.186 align:middle
So that's important.

22:49.186 --> 22:51.706 align:middle
I'm not in any way
connecting it to some sort

22:51.706 --> 22:53.016 align:middle
of near-term market event.

22:53.636 --> 22:56.616 align:middle
But, ultimately, it's something
we'll have to deal with.

22:56.616 --> 23:00.116 align:middle
In the end-in the endgame,
that's where you wind up is

23:00.516 --> 23:02.016 align:middle
in some kind of a
difficult thing.

23:02.016 --> 23:03.966 align:middle
But that's not where we
are-it's not where Japan is,

23:03.966 --> 23:06.786 align:middle
either-but it's certainly
not where we are right now.

23:07.336 --> 23:11.256 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 13

23:11.366 --> 23:13.156 align:middle
of 24 VICTORIA GUIDA.

23:13.156 --> 23:15.216 align:middle
Does it reduce the
effect of your rate cuts

23:15.216 --> 23:16.456 align:middle
that longer-term rates have
overall not really budged

23:16.596 --> 23:16.956 align:middle
that much?

23:16.956 --> 23:17.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:17.166 --> 23:18.016 align:middle
I wouldn't say that.

23:18.016 --> 23:18.876 align:middle
The thing is-I mean,
technically,

23:19.946 --> 23:22.056 align:middle
higher longer-term rates
means less economic

23:22.056 --> 23:22.826 align:middle
financial conditions.

23:22.896 --> 23:26.646 align:middle
But remember-many, many
things move longer-term rates.

23:26.646 --> 23:29.826 align:middle
It's not-and it's not mostly
what happens on the short end.

23:29.826 --> 23:32.256 align:middle
There can be effects
of longer-term rates

23:32.256 --> 23:34.586 align:middle
from our moving our
policy around,

23:34.996 --> 23:39.236 align:middle
but it's much more
assessments of the fiscal path,

23:39.236 --> 23:43.046 align:middle
and fiscal policies, and
risks and things like that

23:43.046 --> 23:44.936 align:middle
that move the 10-year around.

23:44.936 --> 23:46.786 align:middle
You can look back
and find periods

23:46.786 --> 23:49.936 align:middle
where we've been very
actively moving the policy rate

23:50.416 --> 23:53.046 align:middle
over the course of a year, and
over the course of that year,

23:53.346 --> 23:55.056 align:middle
the 10-year is exactly
where it started.

23:55.516 --> 24:00.536 align:middle
So it's not-there's not a tight
link between 10-year rates

24:00.656 --> 24:01.806 align:middle
and the overnight rate.

24:02.196 --> 24:02.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:03.736 --> 24:04.046 align:middle
Elizabeth.

24:04.046 --> 24:06.576 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

24:06.576 --> 24:07.196 align:middle
Thanks so much.

24:07.196 --> 24:08.596 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

24:08.596 --> 24:10.086 align:middle
Republican Senator Thom Tillis,

24:10.086 --> 24:11.696 align:middle
who sits on the Senate
Banking Committee,

24:11.736 --> 24:14.506 align:middle
said he will block any Fed
nominee, including the Chair,

24:15.006 --> 24:17.066 align:middle
until this investigation
into you is resolved.

24:17.466 --> 24:19.156 align:middle
Do you support this
move by the senator,

24:19.156 --> 24:21.866 align:middle
and what conversations have
you had with the senator?

24:21.866 --> 24:22.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:22.346 --> 24:23.356 align:middle
I've got nothing
for you on that.

24:23.356 --> 24:23.946 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

24:23.946 --> 24:27.226 align:middle
More broadly, what would
happen to American households

24:27.406 --> 24:29.626 align:middle
if the Federal Reserve
loses its ability

24:29.686 --> 24:31.966 align:middle
to operate independent
from politics?

24:32.676 --> 24:33.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:34.466 --> 24:38.156 align:middle
So, really, the point
of independence is not

24:38.156 --> 24:40.446 align:middle
to protect policymakers
or anything like that.

24:40.446 --> 24:44.136 align:middle
It just is that every
advanced-economy democracy

24:44.136 --> 24:48.966 align:middle
in the world has come around
to this common practice.

24:48.966 --> 24:50.656 align:middle
It's just an institutional
arrangement

24:50.656 --> 24:52.946 align:middle
that is-that has
served the people well.

24:53.236 --> 24:55.896 align:middle
And that is to have a separation
between-to not have direct

24:56.206 --> 24:57.936 align:middle
elected official
control January 28,

24:57.936 --> 25:00.996 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 14 of 24

25:00.996 --> 25:02.486 align:middle
over the setting
of monetary policy.

25:02.706 --> 25:05.906 align:middle
And the reason is that
monetary policy can be used

25:06.446 --> 25:09.536 align:middle
through an election cycle to
affect the economy in a way

25:09.536 --> 25:11.026 align:middle
that will be politically
worthwhile.

25:11.026 --> 25:13.306 align:middle
So this isn't-I'm not talking
about the U.S. context,

25:13.306 --> 25:16.206 align:middle
this is every advanced-economy
democracy of any size.

25:16.776 --> 25:18.676 align:middle
So it's a good practice,

25:18.676 --> 25:21.956 align:middle
it's pretty much everywhere
among countries that look at all

25:21.956 --> 25:22.846 align:middle
like the United States.

25:23.326 --> 25:25.466 align:middle
And I think if you lose
that, it's-first of all,

25:25.916 --> 25:29.416 align:middle
it would be hard to restore the
credibility of the institution.

25:29.926 --> 25:36.076 align:middle
If people lose their faith that
we're making decisions only

25:36.076 --> 25:40.086 align:middle
on the basis of our assessment
of what's best for everyone,

25:40.086 --> 25:42.956 align:middle
for the wide public, rather
than trying to benefit one group

25:42.956 --> 25:44.886 align:middle
or another-if you lose that,

25:44.886 --> 25:46.166 align:middle
it's going to be
hard to retain it.

25:46.236 --> 25:47.276 align:middle
And we haven't lost it.

25:47.526 --> 25:49.156 align:middle
I don't believe we will.

25:49.366 --> 25:50.386 align:middle
I certainly hope we won't.

25:50.906 --> 25:53.426 align:middle
But it's very important, and
the reason it's important is

25:53.426 --> 25:57.026 align:middle
that it's enabled central banks,
generally, not to be perfect,

25:57.646 --> 25:59.156 align:middle
but to serve the public well.

25:59.156 --> 25:59.666 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

25:59.666 --> 26:02.286 align:middle
You're confident it can maintain
that independence at this point?

26:02.876 --> 26:03.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:03.096 --> 26:05.816 align:middle
Yes. I mean, I'm strongly
committed to that,

26:05.816 --> 26:06.836 align:middle
and so are my colleagues.

26:08.086 --> 26:08.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:12.946 --> 26:13.376 align:middle
Archie. ARCHIE HALL.

26:13.376 --> 26:14.476 align:middle
Archie Hall from the Economist.

26:14.476 --> 26:17.356 align:middle
On that sort of stabilization
of the labor market question,

26:17.446 --> 26:19.556 align:middle
how much do you see
the weakening we saw

26:19.556 --> 26:22.626 align:middle
over the past six months,
year, as a kind of data mirage

26:22.626 --> 26:24.566 align:middle
around immigration, the
government shutdown,

26:24.566 --> 26:26.406 align:middle
and so on, that's now resolved?

26:26.606 --> 26:28.526 align:middle
Or how much have we seen a
kind of real underlying firming

26:28.526 --> 26:30.046 align:middle
up in the state of the
labor market, do you think?

26:30.266 --> 26:30.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:31.936 --> 26:35.756 align:middle
Well, part of it is, to your
point, part of it just is that,

26:36.646 --> 26:40.436 align:middle
that labor supply-growth
in labor supply has come

26:40.436 --> 26:42.996 align:middle
to essentially a halt
from a fairly fast clip

26:42.996 --> 26:46.076 align:middle
of growth January 28, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

26:46.076 --> 26:48.276 align:middle
Page 15 of 24 over the
last couple of years driven

26:48.276 --> 26:50.576 align:middle
by immigration, and then
the halt being driven

26:50.576 --> 26:53.116 align:middle
by a very sudden
stop in immigration.

26:53.206 --> 26:58.066 align:middle
So, many outcomes were
possible with that.

26:58.066 --> 26:59.416 align:middle
Supply came way down.

26:59.416 --> 27:02.006 align:middle
It turns out that demand
for labor also came

27:02.006 --> 27:05.536 align:middle
down a very similar amount,
maybe just a little bit more,

27:05.536 --> 27:07.206 align:middle
which is why the
unemployment rate has gone up.

27:07.206 --> 27:09.556 align:middle
So I don't know whether
that's a coincidence or not,

27:09.556 --> 27:11.956 align:middle
but that's what's
happened with that part.

27:12.296 --> 27:17.416 align:middle
But if you look at other
things, like, for example,

27:17.416 --> 27:21.456 align:middle
the-just to pick a couple-the
Conference Board's measure

27:21.456 --> 27:23.956 align:middle
of job availability that
came out, I don't know,

27:23.956 --> 27:26.266 align:middle
was it yesterday or today?

27:26.266 --> 27:29.816 align:middle
But it shows-it's a survey
showing that workers feel

27:29.816 --> 27:32.176 align:middle
like job availability
is-it's a very low reading.

27:32.426 --> 27:35.346 align:middle
Just one reading, but it's
an indication of softening.

27:35.936 --> 27:39.896 align:middle
People-part-time for economic
reasons, which is a category

27:39.896 --> 27:43.306 align:middle
within the broader
U-6 category measure,

27:44.246 --> 27:45.466 align:middle
has moved up significantly.

27:45.466 --> 27:47.666 align:middle
So there are lots of-and I could
go on and on-there are lots

27:47.666 --> 27:48.996 align:middle
of little places that suggest

27:48.996 --> 27:50.956 align:middle
that the labor market
has softened.

27:51.116 --> 27:55.396 align:middle
But part of-but you're right,
part of payroll job softening is

27:55.396 --> 27:57.566 align:middle
that both the supply and
demand for labor has,

27:58.086 --> 28:00.646 align:middle
has come down-growth in
those two have come down.

28:00.956 --> 28:06.756 align:middle
So that makes it a difficult
time to read the labor market.

28:07.376 --> 28:09.696 align:middle
So imagine they both came
down a lot, to the point

28:09.696 --> 28:10.906 align:middle
where there is no job growth.

28:11.946 --> 28:12.916 align:middle
Is that full employment?

28:13.166 --> 28:14.406 align:middle
In a sense it is.

28:14.406 --> 28:17.966 align:middle
If demand and supply
are in-are in balance,

28:18.696 --> 28:20.296 align:middle
you could say that's
full employment.

28:20.296 --> 28:22.936 align:middle
At the same time, is it-do
we really feel like that's,

28:22.936 --> 28:25.616 align:middle
that's a maximum-employment
economy?

28:25.716 --> 28:27.926 align:middle
It's a challenging-it's
a very challenging

28:27.926 --> 28:29.766 align:middle
and quite unusual situation.

28:30.276 --> 28:30.786 align:middle
ARCHIE HALL.

28:30.956 --> 28:33.296 align:middle
Thanks. And one more-on
growth and the kind

28:33.296 --> 28:34.456 align:middle
of strong growth outlook

28:34.456 --> 28:36.216 align:middle
or strengthening growth
outlook you're now seeing,

28:36.466 --> 28:38.486 align:middle
how much of that is the
fiscal stimulus we're seeing

28:38.486 --> 28:40.536 align:middle
from the Beautiful Bill,
the tax cuts, and all that?

28:40.536 --> 28:41.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:41.026 --> 28:42.546 align:middle
So you're seeing it already.

28:42.546 --> 28:45.286 align:middle
You don't have much of a
fiscal-I think the outlook,

28:45.286 --> 28:47.186 align:middle
you're right, it's
financial conditions

28:47.716 --> 28:48.986 align:middle
and it's fiscal policy for '26.

28:49.256 --> 28:50.036 align:middle
But you've January 28,

28:50.036 --> 28:52.386 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 16

28:52.386 --> 28:55.636 align:middle
of 24 got strong consumption
that's been happening before

28:56.096 --> 28:57.926 align:middle
financial conditions
have been supportive

28:57.926 --> 29:02.226 align:middle
but before the fiscal
effects really are shown.

29:02.226 --> 29:05.886 align:middle
So essentially the economy
has, once again, surprised us

29:05.886 --> 29:07.696 align:middle
with its strength-not
for the first time.

29:08.736 --> 29:11.466 align:middle
Consumer spending,
although it's,

29:11.466 --> 29:13.956 align:middle
it's uneven across
income categories,

29:13.956 --> 29:16.016 align:middle
but consumer spending
overall numbers are good.

29:16.656 --> 29:21.016 align:middle
And we're benefiting from
the-from the AI buildout

29:21.246 --> 29:24.846 align:middle
of data centers-that's another
thing we're benefiting from.

29:24.846 --> 29:28.046 align:middle
But the economy, overall,
growth is-growth is

29:28.046 --> 29:29.966 align:middle
on solid footing, it looks like.

29:29.966 --> 29:31.696 align:middle
And it's not just those things.

29:31.696 --> 29:36.546 align:middle
It's just that consumers-the
consumer is filling out surveys

29:36.586 --> 29:38.726 align:middle
that sound really
negative and then spending.

29:39.386 --> 29:41.246 align:middle
So there's been a
disconnect for some time

29:41.246 --> 29:45.886 align:middle
between downbeat surveys and
reasonably good spending data.

29:46.286 --> 29:46.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:47.406 --> 29:47.626 align:middle
Christine.

29:48.186 --> 29:49.896 align:middle
CHRISTINE ROMANS.

29:50.026 --> 29:50.616 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

29:50.616 --> 29:51.906 align:middle
Christine Romans from NBC News.

29:51.976 --> 29:55.096 align:middle
You talked about how
consumer spending is uneven.

29:55.356 --> 29:58.266 align:middle
The President calls inflation
"defeated" and "solved."

29:58.516 --> 30:01.956 align:middle
The FOMC says it's a
"somewhat elevated" inflation.

30:02.336 --> 30:04.926 align:middle
But you talked about those
customer survey-or those

30:04.986 --> 30:08.226 align:middle
consumer sentiment surveys and
public opinion polls that show

30:08.226 --> 30:10.056 align:middle
that most families say the cost

30:10.056 --> 30:12.096 align:middle
of living is still
issue number one.

30:12.096 --> 30:15.096 align:middle
What is the conversation around
the table with your colleagues

30:15.096 --> 30:19.296 align:middle
about how wealthier consumers
seem to be driving so much

30:19.296 --> 30:23.686 align:middle
of the economy, and why so
many families still feel

30:23.686 --> 30:26.016 align:middle
like they just can't
make ends meet

30:26.016 --> 30:27.846 align:middle
after five years
of rising prices?

30:27.846 --> 30:28.916 align:middle
What is that discussion like?

30:29.436 --> 30:29.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:29.796 --> 30:30.696 align:middle
So a couple things.

30:30.696 --> 30:34.306 align:middle
One, there's something,
something to it in that we know

30:34.306 --> 30:39.756 align:middle
that higher-income households
tend to own real estate and tend

30:39.756 --> 30:41.976 align:middle
to own stocks, stocks
and securities,

30:42.446 --> 30:46.036 align:middle
and those assets have been
going up in value, and increases

30:46.036 --> 30:48.716 align:middle
in wealth do support
spending over time,

30:48.866 --> 30:51.686 align:middle
so-and that's clearly
a part of the story.

30:52.146 --> 30:54.646 align:middle
We also know that for some
time now, for a year or more,

30:54.646 --> 30:56.136 align:middle
we've been hearing from
retailers, for example,

30:56.136 --> 30:57.626 align:middle
that serve lower-income
January 28,

30:57.626 --> 31:01.246 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 17

31:01.246 --> 31:04.986 align:middle
of 24 customers, whether it
be food or the big-box stores

31:04.986 --> 31:07.296 align:middle
or any-they're saying
the same thing, which is,

31:07.686 --> 31:11.256 align:middle
"Our consumers are
looking to economize.

31:11.406 --> 31:14.706 align:middle
They're trading down from
brands, and they're buying less

31:14.706 --> 31:17.696 align:middle
and changing their buying
habits and that kind of thing."

31:17.696 --> 31:18.616 align:middle
So we're seeing that,

31:18.616 --> 31:21.866 align:middle
and that's-that is a
reality of what we're seeing.

31:21.866 --> 31:22.876 align:middle
They're still consuming,

31:23.406 --> 31:26.476 align:middle
but they're feeling
it in a different way.

31:26.666 --> 31:29.986 align:middle
I would say more
broadly, on affordability,

31:29.986 --> 31:33.306 align:middle
we have a vast network
through the Reserve Banks,

31:33.306 --> 31:35.816 align:middle
and also through the Board
of Governors, where we talk

31:35.816 --> 31:38.736 align:middle
to small and large
businesses and households.

31:39.316 --> 31:42.196 align:middle
So we do hear a lot
about affordability,

31:42.196 --> 31:44.016 align:middle
and we take-we take
that very seriously.

31:44.016 --> 31:47.356 align:middle
And we take it to heart
because our job is-one

31:47.356 --> 31:51.606 align:middle
of our jobs is price stability,
and so the best thing we can do

31:51.696 --> 31:55.236 align:middle
for people who are feeling that
squeeze is to keep inflation

31:55.236 --> 31:57.466 align:middle
under control and,
frankly, to finish the job

31:57.466 --> 31:59.226 align:middle
of getting inflation
back down to 2 percent.

31:59.296 --> 32:00.006 align:middle
CHRISTINE ROMANS.

32:00.006 --> 32:01.866 align:middle
You mentioned the AI
buildout as being positive

32:01.866 --> 32:03.536 align:middle
for economic growth this year.

32:03.866 --> 32:04.706 align:middle
I wonder as you look

32:04.706 --> 32:07.056 align:middle
at the weakest year-last
year-for job creation

32:07.056 --> 32:10.896 align:middle
of a nonrecession year since,
like, 2003, are you concerned

32:10.896 --> 32:15.336 align:middle
about AI maybe supplanting
more entry-level work

32:15.336 --> 32:16.606 align:middle
and entry-level jobs?

32:16.606 --> 32:18.966 align:middle
And how does that play into
your-what you're watching

32:18.966 --> 32:19.736 align:middle
about the labor market?

32:19.736 --> 32:20.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:20.826 --> 32:24.456 align:middle
So everyone, of course, is
watching AI and the deployment

32:24.456 --> 32:27.986 align:middle
and trying to understand
exactly what's happening,

32:28.516 --> 32:31.116 align:middle
and there's a wide
range of possibilities.

32:31.116 --> 32:35.326 align:middle
It's hard to say, and of course
anyone who uses it is amazed

32:35.326 --> 32:36.926 align:middle
at what it can accomplish,
right?

32:37.576 --> 32:42.646 align:middle
So every technological wave
will eliminate some jobs

32:42.646 --> 32:45.716 align:middle
and create other jobs, and
it's always been the case.

32:45.716 --> 32:48.216 align:middle
If you look back, wave
after wave after wave,

32:48.216 --> 32:49.446 align:middle
there will be some disruption,

32:49.446 --> 32:52.796 align:middle
but ultimately technology
increases productivity,

32:52.796 --> 32:55.256 align:middle
which is the basis
for rising wages.

32:55.256 --> 32:57.306 align:middle
And it may not all
happen immediately,

32:57.306 --> 33:00.996 align:middle
but over time it's what-it's
what enables incomes to rise

33:00.996 --> 33:02.486 align:middle
over time is rising January 28,

33:02.486 --> 33:06.096 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 18

33:06.096 --> 33:07.066 align:middle
of 24 productivity.

33:07.066 --> 33:08.736 align:middle
And we always ask, well this
is going to be different,

33:08.736 --> 33:09.526 align:middle
is it going to be different?

33:09.526 --> 33:10.696 align:middle
And we don't know.

33:10.846 --> 33:13.006 align:middle
And we may, in any case,
see, in the short term,

33:13.586 --> 33:16.576 align:middle
jobs that are being eliminated
by the capabilities of AI.

33:16.576 --> 33:17.416 align:middle
We may see that.

33:17.416 --> 33:21.006 align:middle
We just don't know what the
overall effect is going to be.

33:21.106 --> 33:23.786 align:middle
So how to think about
it-in macroeconomic terms,

33:23.786 --> 33:24.676 align:middle
it's very hard.

33:24.676 --> 33:27.376 align:middle
We can look at the
aggregate data.

33:27.376 --> 33:28.676 align:middle
We can analyze,

33:28.676 --> 33:32.976 align:middle
for example-there is some
connection, it appears,

33:32.976 --> 33:38.556 align:middle
between the low hiring rate for
recent college grads and AI,

33:38.756 --> 33:43.906 align:middle
but it's not the
main, or only, driver.

33:43.986 --> 33:45.326 align:middle
You hear large companies,
though,

33:45.326 --> 33:47.976 align:middle
saying-many of them saying-that
they either won't be hiring

33:47.976 --> 33:50.376 align:middle
for some time, or that
they're hiring less,

33:50.376 --> 33:53.306 align:middle
or that they're laying people
off, and they tend to refer

33:53.306 --> 33:55.696 align:middle
to AI when they do that.

33:55.696 --> 33:57.706 align:middle
So we're all watching
and learning,

33:58.006 --> 34:00.746 align:middle
and it could-it could certainly
have pretty significant effects

34:00.746 --> 34:02.966 align:middle
on the economy, the
workforce, and our society.

34:02.966 --> 34:05.696 align:middle
We don't really have the tools

34:05.696 --> 34:08.136 align:middle
to address the concerns
that may arise.

34:08.726 --> 34:12.176 align:middle
But we have a lot of people
who focus on analyzing it

34:12.266 --> 34:15.746 align:middle
and try to-try to understand
what the macroeconomic

34:15.746 --> 34:17.226 align:middle
implications are,
which is our job.

34:17.226 --> 34:19.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:19.146 --> 34:20.986 align:middle
Beiyi. BEIYI SEOW.

34:23.596 --> 34:24.586 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

34:24.656 --> 34:26.616 align:middle
Beiyi from AFP News Agency.

34:27.606 --> 34:29.776 align:middle
You mentioned earlier
that on inflation,

34:29.776 --> 34:33.646 align:middle
the broad expectation was for a
one-time price increase and then

34:33.646 --> 34:34.766 align:middle
for inflation to come down.

34:34.766 --> 34:37.996 align:middle
I was wondering, is your
expectation still for inflation

34:37.996 --> 34:41.036 align:middle
to start cooling in the
second half of 2026?

34:41.036 --> 34:43.146 align:middle
And if you could
elaborate how far we are

34:43.236 --> 34:44.346 align:middle
from target currently.

34:44.496 --> 34:44.806 align:middle
Thank you.

34:45.436 --> 34:45.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:45.986 --> 34:48.966 align:middle
So how far we are from
target is, as I mentioned

34:48.966 --> 34:53.186 align:middle
in my opening remarks, we're-we
had 3 percent-3.0 percent core

34:53.186 --> 34:56.686 align:middle
PCE inflation over the 12
months ending in December.

34:56.686 --> 34:58.486 align:middle
And that's pretty much what
we had the year before.

34:58.486 --> 35:00.196 align:middle
So, on net, no progress.

35:00.196 --> 35:02.766 align:middle
But January 28, 2026 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

35:02.766 --> 35:04.766 align:middle
Page 19 of 24 the story behind
that is modestly positive

35:04.766 --> 35:07.086 align:middle
in that most of the
overshoot was in goods prices,

35:07.406 --> 35:08.886 align:middle
which we think is
related to tariffs,

35:09.266 --> 35:11.936 align:middle
and ultimately we think those
will not result in inflation,

35:11.936 --> 35:14.066 align:middle
as opposed to a one-time
price increase.

35:14.356 --> 35:17.156 align:middle
Okay, so that's where it is.

35:17.156 --> 35:25.326 align:middle
In terms of, you asked-so no
one thinks they will, ex ante,

35:25.326 --> 35:28.596 align:middle
understand really clearly,
precisely when this will happen.

35:28.936 --> 35:32.956 align:middle
But there's an expectation that
sometime in the middle quarters

35:32.956 --> 35:37.796 align:middle
of the year, we'll see
tariff inflation topping out.

35:38.166 --> 35:41.456 align:middle
So what we do is, we-when
a tariff is put in place,

35:41.906 --> 35:45.986 align:middle
we track the effect of those
tariffs over a six-, seven-,

35:45.986 --> 35:49.416 align:middle
eight-, nine-month period, and
you can see-and then you can see

35:49.496 --> 35:52.636 align:middle
for that tariff how long
it takes to reach a place

35:52.636 --> 35:55.546 align:middle
where it's affected the
price level and that's it.

35:55.736 --> 35:58.416 align:middle
So we're getting better at
that, and our estimate is

35:58.416 --> 36:00.146 align:middle
that it'll be sometime in
the middle of the year,

36:00.146 --> 36:02.436 align:middle
but I wouldn't look for
great precision in that.

36:02.526 --> 36:05.006 align:middle
But we'll be able to see
whether things are moving

36:05.006 --> 36:05.696 align:middle
in that direction.

36:05.696 --> 36:07.766 align:middle
I think we'll be able to see.

36:07.766 --> 36:09.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:09.906 --> 36:11.006 align:middle
Richard Escobedo.

36:11.736 --> 36:13.866 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

36:14.716 --> 36:15.486 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

36:15.486 --> 36:17.126 align:middle
I'm Richard Escobedo
with CBS News.

36:17.476 --> 36:19.416 align:middle
I want to look outside the U.S.

36:19.416 --> 36:21.546 align:middle
about what's happening
outside our shores

36:21.546 --> 36:24.556 align:middle
and how it impacts the U.S.
Canada's prime minister said

36:24.556 --> 36:27.446 align:middle
last week that there's been a
"rupture in the global order,"

36:27.446 --> 36:29.916 align:middle
and I wonder how you're
thinking about geopolitical risk

36:29.916 --> 36:31.906 align:middle
as it relates to
the U.S. economy.

36:31.906 --> 36:32.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:32.586 --> 36:36.766 align:middle
So I can't-I can't comment
on that speech or statement

36:36.766 --> 36:37.606 align:middle
or anything like that.

36:37.806 --> 36:43.276 align:middle
Geopolitical risk for us is-a
lot of it is around energy, oil.

36:44.066 --> 36:46.626 align:middle
And so far we haven't-for
all the turmoil,

36:46.626 --> 36:49.406 align:middle
we really-oil prices have
come down, as you know,

36:49.486 --> 36:51.106 align:middle
and so we don't really see much.

36:51.976 --> 36:55.036 align:middle
Longer than that, it's trade,

36:55.036 --> 37:00.786 align:middle
and the trade-the
economy-our economy has pulled

37:00.786 --> 37:02.026 align:middle
through pretty well,
you'd have to say,

37:02.026 --> 37:04.786 align:middle
given the very significant
changes in trade policy.

37:04.866 --> 37:07.566 align:middle
The U.S. economy has,
has pushed right through.

37:07.566 --> 37:09.226 align:middle
Partly that is-that the way
that what was January 28,

37:09.226 --> 37:11.126 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 20

37:11.126 --> 37:12.956 align:middle
of 24 implemented was
significantly less

37:12.956 --> 37:14.996 align:middle
than what was announced
at the beginning.

37:15.406 --> 37:18.826 align:middle
In addition, other countries
didn't retaliate, and,

37:18.826 --> 37:21.446 align:middle
in addition, a good part
of it hasn't been passed

37:21.446 --> 37:22.276 align:middle
through to consumers yet.

37:22.276 --> 37:25.316 align:middle
It's being-it's being taken
by companies that stand

37:25.316 --> 37:27.576 align:middle
between the consumer
and the exporter.

37:28.526 --> 37:30.476 align:middle
So that's where that is.

37:31.106 --> 37:31.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:31.916 --> 37:35.516 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

37:35.666 --> 37:36.456 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

37:36.456 --> 37:38.396 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

37:38.686 --> 37:41.656 align:middle
With third-quarter GDP
growing at 4.4 percent

37:41.656 --> 37:45.496 align:middle
and the fourth quarter expected
to have a five handle on it,

37:45.496 --> 37:48.106 align:middle
as to what the Atlanta
Fed is predicting,

37:48.386 --> 37:50.026 align:middle
at a time when you had
a government shutdown

37:50.026 --> 37:51.986 align:middle
and we thought that was going
to shave off some growth.

37:52.356 --> 37:54.416 align:middle
You've also got the fiscal
tailwinds, you've talked

37:54.416 --> 37:58.356 align:middle
about big tax refunds coming,
potential tariff dividend.

37:58.936 --> 38:01.966 align:middle
How could you cut rates
and not spur inflation

38:01.966 --> 38:02.786 align:middle
in that environment?

38:02.786 --> 38:03.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:04.286 --> 38:06.106 align:middle
Well, we didn't cut rates today.

38:06.706 --> 38:12.606 align:middle
But it depends on how fast
potential output is growing,

38:12.746 --> 38:12.936 align:middle
right?

38:13.326 --> 38:14.926 align:middle
I'm just asking in principle.

38:14.926 --> 38:17.406 align:middle
I'm not-I'm not saying
this is what's happening.

38:17.726 --> 38:20.636 align:middle
There's growth, and there's how
fast the potential is growing.

38:21.136 --> 38:25.656 align:middle
And in a time of high
productivity growth,

38:25.716 --> 38:27.026 align:middle
potential output is rising.

38:27.026 --> 38:32.016 align:middle
So it really matters whether
potential output is growing

38:32.016 --> 38:33.436 align:middle
as fast as actual output.

38:33.436 --> 38:34.916 align:middle
And it would matter over time.

38:35.236 --> 38:36.956 align:middle
The numbers you cite
were for quarters,

38:36.956 --> 38:40.406 align:middle
and quarterly GDP is-you
need to look at 12 months,

38:40.406 --> 38:42.706 align:middle
because quarterly GDP
can be very lumpy.

38:43.046 --> 38:44.386 align:middle
Quarterly GDP [growth]
was negative

38:44.386 --> 38:45.516 align:middle
in the first quarter last year.

38:45.766 --> 38:47.676 align:middle
So the overall-over the year,

38:47.676 --> 38:48.886 align:middle
the numbers were
nothing like that.

38:48.886 --> 38:51.476 align:middle
It was more in the
mid-2s for the year.

38:51.876 --> 38:52.476 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

38:52.476 --> 38:55.156 align:middle
And how do you explain
the divide right now

38:55.186 --> 38:58.336 align:middle
between strong economic
growth and the job market?

38:58.336 --> 38:59.736 align:middle
Is it productivity
that's filling the gap?

38:59.736 --> 39:01.236 align:middle
And is productivity being
driven by AI at this point?

39:01.236 --> 39:04.296 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 21

39:04.296 --> 39:05.126 align:middle
of 24 CHAIR POWELL.

39:05.126 --> 39:09.596 align:middle
So we-there has-you're right,
there has been a divide

39:09.596 --> 39:13.196 align:middle
of solid growth but what looked
like a weakening labor market,

39:13.966 --> 39:19.486 align:middle
and that can be explained
by rising productivity.

39:20.186 --> 39:23.376 align:middle
But I would say we do
see signs, certainly,

39:23.526 --> 39:27.506 align:middle
of the unemployment
rate stabilizing.

39:27.996 --> 39:30.186 align:middle
So it may be we're
seeing the beginning

39:30.186 --> 39:32.456 align:middle
of the resolution
of those two things.

39:32.666 --> 39:36.806 align:middle
Also, as you probably know,
the lore is that when GDP

39:36.806 --> 39:41.296 align:middle
and the labor market get
into an argument, in the end,

39:41.786 --> 39:44.196 align:middle
labor market is more-the labor
market data is more reliable.

39:44.196 --> 39:47.616 align:middle
GDP data is very hard to
collect and understand.

39:47.616 --> 39:51.196 align:middle
But, nonetheless, I
think we may be seeing

39:51.196 --> 39:53.856 align:middle
that tension resolving a
little bit-too soon to say

39:53.856 --> 39:55.606 align:middle
with any confidence, though.

39:56.446 --> 39:56.936 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:58.216 --> 39:59.116 align:middle
Matt Egan.

40:00.946 --> 40:01.396 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

40:01.396 --> 40:02.456 align:middle
Matt Egan with CNN.

40:02.516 --> 40:06.106 align:middle
Chair Powell, after today
you have two meetings left

40:06.226 --> 40:07.516 align:middle
as Fed Chair.

40:07.916 --> 40:10.446 align:middle
You've obviously experienced
a lot during your time

40:10.446 --> 40:12.896 align:middle
as Fed Chair, served
under multiple Presidents.

40:12.896 --> 40:14.756 align:middle
I'm wondering what
advice you have

40:15.186 --> 40:17.116 align:middle
for whoever your
successor might be.

40:19.626 --> 40:20.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:22.026 --> 40:23.366 align:middle
Honestly, I'd say
a couple things.

40:23.366 --> 40:29.616 align:middle
One is stay out of
elected politics.

40:29.846 --> 40:31.646 align:middle
Don't get pulled into
elected politics.

40:32.546 --> 40:33.436 align:middle
Don't do it.

40:33.566 --> 40:35.546 align:middle
That's another thing.

40:36.306 --> 40:39.976 align:middle
Another is that our window

40:39.976 --> 40:44.386 align:middle
into democratic accountability
is Congress.

40:45.216 --> 40:49.116 align:middle
And it's not a passive
burden for us to go

40:49.116 --> 40:51.106 align:middle
to Congress and talk to people.

40:51.106 --> 40:53.326 align:middle
It's an affirmative,
regular obligation.

40:53.916 --> 40:55.906 align:middle
If you want democratic
legitimacy,

40:55.956 --> 41:00.506 align:middle
you earn it by your interactions
with our elected overseers.

41:00.506 --> 41:03.796 align:middle
And so it's something
you need to work hard at,

41:03.796 --> 41:06.126 align:middle
and I have worked hard at it.

41:06.126 --> 41:09.466 align:middle
And the last thing is,
it's easy to-it's easy

41:09.466 --> 41:12.286 align:middle
to criticize government
institutions in so many ways.

41:12.876 --> 41:14.216 align:middle
I will tell whoever it is,

41:14.346 --> 41:16.216 align:middle
you're about to meet
the most qualified group

41:16.216 --> 41:18.676 align:middle
of people you not
only have ever worked

41:18.676 --> 41:20.076 align:middle
with [but also that]
you will ever work with,

41:20.706 --> 41:22.006 align:middle
when you meet Fed staff.

41:22.646 --> 41:26.336 align:middle
And not everybody's perfect,
but there isn't a better cadre

41:26.336 --> 41:28.206 align:middle
of professionals more dedicated

41:28.596 --> 41:30.976 align:middle
to the public well-being
than work at the Fed.

41:30.976 --> 41:34.366 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 22

41:34.366 --> 41:35.066 align:middle
of 24 MATT EGAN.

41:35.066 --> 41:36.076 align:middle
Thanks for that answer.

41:36.126 --> 41:37.276 align:middle
If I may follow up-as
I'm sure you've noticed,

41:37.276 --> 41:40.886 align:middle
gold and silver prices have
experienced historic gains

41:40.886 --> 41:41.346 align:middle
of late.

41:41.766 --> 41:44.296 align:middle
And I'm wondering how
much attention, if any,

41:44.296 --> 41:48.446 align:middle
you pay to those moves, and
what message you may take

41:48.446 --> 41:51.196 align:middle
from these significant
price increases we've seen

41:51.196 --> 41:52.666 align:middle
for precious metals.

41:53.076 --> 41:53.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:53.496 --> 41:55.626 align:middle
Don't take much message
macroeconomically.

41:55.936 --> 42:00.606 align:middle
The argument can be made
that we're losing credibility

42:00.606 --> 42:01.946 align:middle
or something-it's
simply not the case.

42:01.946 --> 42:04.486 align:middle
If you look-if you look at where
inflation expectations are,

42:04.956 --> 42:06.736 align:middle
our credibility is right
where it needs to be.

42:07.106 --> 42:09.646 align:middle
So we look at those
things, we don't get spun

42:09.646 --> 42:12.086 align:middle
up over particular
asset price changes,

42:12.086 --> 42:14.826 align:middle
although we do monitor
them, of course.

42:16.176 --> 42:17.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:17.386 --> 42:18.596 align:middle
Nicole for the last question.

42:21.116 --> 42:21.476 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

42:21.756 --> 42:23.206 align:middle
Hi. Nicole Goodkind, Barron's.

42:24.106 --> 42:26.306 align:middle
Some prominent critics
have charged

42:26.306 --> 42:30.126 align:middle
that the Fed's economic models
are somewhat backward looking

42:30.576 --> 42:32.846 align:middle
but should be more forward
looking, incorporating things

42:32.846 --> 42:34.766 align:middle
like productivity
increases from AI.

42:35.456 --> 42:38.356 align:middle
How do you incorporate
current and future developments

42:38.356 --> 42:39.816 align:middle
into your analysis
and decisionmaking,

42:39.816 --> 42:42.506 align:middle
and do you have an
answer to those critics?

42:43.306 --> 42:43.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:44.016 --> 42:48.846 align:middle
Yeah, so by and large,
those criticisms,

42:49.546 --> 42:51.756 align:middle
as somebody on the inside-they
just don't make sense,

42:51.756 --> 42:52.386 align:middle
and I'll tell you why.

42:53.846 --> 42:56.416 align:middle
Every FOMC participant writes

42:56.416 --> 42:59.016 align:middle
down a forecast every
quarter, right?

42:59.066 --> 43:00.556 align:middle
The Summary of Economic
Projections.

43:00.806 --> 43:03.706 align:middle
And that's the basis for how
we think about the economy.

43:03.706 --> 43:11.606 align:middle
And the other thing is, what an
economic model can do is it can

43:11.676 --> 43:14.616 align:middle
grind up all the data for
the last number of years,

43:14.616 --> 43:15.736 align:middle
50 years let's say,

43:16.356 --> 43:19.196 align:middle
and it can-it can identify
what are the relationships

43:19.196 --> 43:22.836 align:middle
between variables A, B, C, D,
and all that kind of thing.

43:22.836 --> 43:25.356 align:middle
And it can tell you if you
change one of those variables,

43:25.496 --> 43:27.316 align:middle
this is what should
happen in the macroeconomy.

43:27.546 --> 43:28.786 align:middle
That's just the way it works.

43:28.786 --> 43:30.676 align:middle
However, the structure of the
macroeconomy is January 28,

43:30.676 --> 43:32.716 align:middle
2026 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 23

43:32.716 --> 43:33.536 align:middle
of 24 constantly changing.

43:33.536 --> 43:35.286 align:middle
For example, we hadn't had
a pandemic in 100 years.

43:35.676 --> 43:36.866 align:middle
It wasn't in the model.

43:37.206 --> 43:38.996 align:middle
And we knew it from the
very beginning-it was not

43:39.116 --> 43:39.766 align:middle
in the model.

43:40.296 --> 43:45.146 align:middle
A trade war of this scope-we'd
never had that in 100 years.

43:45.786 --> 43:50.426 align:middle
And so there's great
uncertainty at different points.

43:50.946 --> 43:56.126 align:middle
Another thing I'll
say is, when it comes

43:57.016 --> 44:01.576 align:middle
to technological developments
that raise potential output,

44:01.736 --> 44:05.886 align:middle
some kind of technological
revolution like happened

44:05.886 --> 44:08.046 align:middle
in the '90s here and
like may be happening now

44:08.046 --> 44:10.476 align:middle
with AI-we're all over that.

44:10.626 --> 44:13.936 align:middle
Everyone studies those periods,
and we're very clear-eyed

44:14.026 --> 44:17.616 align:middle
about the possibility that this
higher productivity may persist,

44:18.296 --> 44:19.766 align:middle
and also that it may not.

44:20.016 --> 44:25.036 align:middle
We're not-no one's sitting
here unaware of the possibility

44:25.036 --> 44:25.816 align:middle
of higher productivity.

44:25.816 --> 44:27.176 align:middle
We've been talking about
it for three years.

44:27.526 --> 44:30.086 align:middle
It long predates the current
situation-it's been going

44:30.086 --> 44:33.216 align:middle
on for five or six years
we've had productivity higher.

44:33.496 --> 44:34.846 align:middle
We've been talking about
it that whole time.

44:35.306 --> 44:36.966 align:middle
So it's very much on our minds.

44:36.966 --> 44:38.616 align:middle
And we are well aware

44:39.176 --> 44:41.676 align:middle
that higher productivity
means higher potential output,

44:42.166 --> 44:44.846 align:middle
and it changes the way you
think about, potentially,

44:44.846 --> 44:47.246 align:middle
inflation growth, labor
market, and all those things.

44:47.576 --> 44:48.766 align:middle
That's all in our models.

44:49.366 --> 44:50.746 align:middle
I mean, if it's a question

44:50.746 --> 44:52.586 align:middle
of using better models,
bring them on.

44:52.786 --> 44:53.386 align:middle
Where are they?

44:53.386 --> 44:53.876 align:middle
We'll take them.

44:54.126 --> 44:58.326 align:middle
But I think we certainly
are in contact with anybody

44:58.326 --> 44:59.606 align:middle
who does economic modeling,

45:00.206 --> 45:02.246 align:middle
and we're always looking
to do better at that.

45:02.246 --> 45:03.406 align:middle
But that's how I
think about that.

45:04.166 --> 45:04.556 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

45:04.556 --> 45:07.476 align:middle
And just a quick follow-up:
We've been talking a lot

45:07.476 --> 45:10.246 align:middle
about tariffs passing through,
and we've been talking a lot

45:10.246 --> 45:12.026 align:middle
about them for the
past few months.

45:12.556 --> 45:16.016 align:middle
The trade landscape is still
in a constant state of flux.

45:16.176 --> 45:17.406 align:middle
Announcements, threats,

45:17.406 --> 45:19.436 align:middle
negotiations-they're
all frequently changing.

45:19.436 --> 45:22.026 align:middle
So I'm wondering how you
actually track these.

45:22.026 --> 45:23.326 align:middle
How-what data or
channels are most critical

45:23.326 --> 45:24.426 align:middle
to follow this in real time?

45:24.426 --> 45:24.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:24.736 --> 45:25.406 align:middle
Trade? NICOLE GOODKIND.

45:25.406 --> 45:26.906 align:middle
Yeah, the impact of tariffs
and how they're changing.

45:27.206 --> 45:31.056 align:middle
January 28, 2026 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 24

45:31.136 --> 45:32.446 align:middle
of 24 CHAIR POWELL.

45:32.446 --> 45:35.006 align:middle
Yeah, I think our staff has
done a really nice job on that,

45:35.006 --> 45:37.866 align:middle
and they kind of put it
together in real time.

45:37.936 --> 45:40.566 align:middle
So as I mentioned, a
tariff gets put in place,

45:40.566 --> 45:42.306 align:middle
you can pretty much
track its effects

45:42.736 --> 45:45.136 align:middle
on pricing and on everything.

45:45.246 --> 45:50.126 align:middle
And so you build a model up from
all of the-all of the tariffs.

45:50.586 --> 45:53.326 align:middle
At the beginning, it was
very much of a forecast; now,

45:53.476 --> 45:56.656 align:middle
it's-every, every
cycle that goes by,

45:56.656 --> 45:58.756 align:middle
it becomes more informed
by actual data.

45:59.436 --> 46:03.596 align:middle
And we were-we-our
forecasts were not far off.

46:04.166 --> 46:06.786 align:middle
What changed was, as I
think I said earlier,

46:06.786 --> 46:09.506 align:middle
what changed was what was
implemented was smaller

46:09.506 --> 46:10.496 align:middle
than what was announced.

46:11.156 --> 46:13.536 align:middle
In addition, we didn't see
retaliation internationally,

46:13.536 --> 46:15.316 align:middle
and I think people
did generally expect

46:15.316 --> 46:16.926 align:middle
that because we saw
that in the past.

46:17.326 --> 46:18.976 align:middle
And that really mattered too.

46:19.426 --> 46:22.006 align:middle
And then the other thing is the
pass-through-didn't know how

46:22.006 --> 46:23.636 align:middle
fast that was going
to be to consumers,

46:23.636 --> 46:28.376 align:middle
didn't know how much exporters
would take, how much companies

46:28.376 --> 46:29.256 align:middle
in the middle would take,

46:29.256 --> 46:30.546 align:middle
and how much the
consumer would take.

46:30.546 --> 46:33.116 align:middle
And it turns out it's a lot of
companies in the middle-who,

46:33.116 --> 46:36.826 align:middle
by the way, are pretty strongly
committed to passing the rest

46:36.826 --> 46:38.976 align:middle
of it through, which is one
of the reasons why we need

46:38.976 --> 46:42.446 align:middle
to keep our eye on inflation and
not declare victory prematurely.

46:42.446 --> 46:42.966 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:43.806 --> 46:44.126 align:middle
Thank you.

46:45.356 --> 46:45.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:46.066 --> 46:46.386 align:middle
We're done?

46:46.606 --> 46:47.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:47.986 --> 46:48.336 align:middle
We're done.

46:48.336 --> 46:49.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:49.156 --> 46:52.566 align:middle
Okay. Thank you very much.

